      "final_text": "Done. `tools/minidiff.py` is a stdlib-only Python 3 script (imports just `difflib`, `re`, `sys` \u2014 verified programmatically against `sys.stdlib_module_names`). It diffs line-by-line, and for lines that were modified in place it runs a second token-level pass so changed words are bracketed: `[-removed-]` on the `-` side, `{+added+}` on the `+` side. Only changed hunks print, with 1 line of context.\n\n**Fixtures:** `a.txt` holds 5 lines of lighthouse prose; `b.txt` is identical except for exactly 3 single-word substitutions \u2014 `rocky`\u2192`granite` (line 1), `seven`\u2192`nine` (line 2), `Gulls`\u2192`Terns` (line 3).\n\n**Verification I ran:**\n- All 3 substituted words plus their 3 originals appear in the output; the unchanged line 5 (`...find the harbor.`) is correctly omitted, confirming context is capped at 1 line.\n- Exit code is **1** on the differing fixtures and **0** on a file compared against a copy of itself.\n- Hunk header `@@ -1,4 +1,4 @@` is byte-identical to what GNU `diff -U1` produces on the same fixtures, and matched GNU on three further cases: a mid-file change, a pure deletion, and an insertion at the top of the file.\n- Error paths: wrong argument count and a nonexistent file both exit 2 with a message on stderr; two empty files exit 0.\n\nThe tool behaved correctly on the first run, so no debugging or fixes were needed. This is a command-line tool with no web surface, so browser verification did not apply; I verified through direct CLI execution and the GNU `diff` cross-check instead.\n\nExact diff output between the two fixtures:\n\n```\n--- a.txt\n+++ b.txt\n@@ -1,4 +1,4 @@\n-The lighthouse stood alone on the [-rocky-] headland above the bay.\n+The lighthouse stood alone on the {+granite+} headland above the bay.\n-Its lamp turned through the fog every [-seven-] seconds without fail.\n+Its lamp turned through the fog every {+nine+} seconds without fail.\n-[-Gulls-] circled the gallery rail where the keeper once walked at dusk.\n+{+Terns+} circled the gallery rail where the keeper once walked at dusk.\n Salt wind scoured the white paint from the seaward wall each winter.\n```"
